From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christophe Pettus <xof@thebuild.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 12:50:15 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 24/35] H14: refuse JBIG2 decodes over the memory budget as
 ImageMemoryLimit

hayro-syntax decodes JBIG2 images with a memory budget of
JBIG2_BUDGET_BYTES_PER_PIXEL (2) bytes per pixel of
DecodeLimits::max_image_pixels; no cap, no budget. A refusal by the
budget is the new DecodeFailure::ImageMemoryLimit, counted once in the
thread-local image_memory_limit_hits(); every other codec error stays
ImageDecode. JPX_BUDGET_BYTES_PER_PIXEL (64) is defined alongside for the
JPEG 2000 budget. hayro-interpret raises the new
InterpreterWarning::ImageMemoryLimit for it, instead of
ImageDecodeFailure or ImagePixelLimit, never as well.

Core and the render crate surface it as WarningKind::ImageMemoryLimit
(category "image", detail "memory limit: ..."), sampled in the same
signal window as the pixel-limit counter: the larger of the
thread-local delta and the interpreter sink's count. A refused image
draws nothing; the page is otherwise rendered and recognised.

The test kit gains jbig2: hand-assembled embedded streams in the shapes
that exhaust the decoder (built from Huffman-coded dictionaries and
text regions, uncompressed and all-white MMR bitmaps, so no entropy
coder is needed), and the symbol-export bomb as a PDF, also a Python
hostile fixture. Tests at every layer: crates/core/tests/h14_jbig2_budget.rs
(the codec, hayro-syntax, hayro-interpret and core's open path, with
peak RSS of the two largest refusals measured in a child process), the
render and OCR warnings, the text path, and Python.
